5. BSM-level anomaly test

Given a BSM from sender s to receiver r:

Compute d_claimed.

Find bin k = floor(d_claimed) (cap to [0, maxD]).

Look up CI_low(k), CI_high(k) for the chosen confidence level (99.7%).

If rssi < CI_low(k) or rssi > CI_high(k), mark BSM = anomalous; otherwise BSM = normal. 

Physical Layer Plausibility Che…

In MATLAB:

function is_anom = isBSMAnomalous(d_claimed, rssi, CI_low, CI_high)
    k = floor(d_claimed);
    k = max(0,min(k, numel(CI_low)-1));  % clamp
    k_idx = k + 1;  % MATLAB index
    if isnan(CI_low(k_idx))
        is_anom = false; % no model -> treat as normal or ignore
        return;
    end
    is_anom = (rssi < CI_low(k_idx)) || (rssi > CI_high(k_idx));
end

6. Vehicle-level plausibility checks

The three algorithms operate on sequences of BSMs from a given sender to a given receiver. 

Physical Layer Plausibility Che…

You should group your data by (receiver_id, sender_id) (each receiver runs its own detection).

Let a_n ∈ {0,1} be the anomaly indicator for the nth BSM in that pair (1 = anomalous, 0 = normal). Equivalently, BSMscore_n = 1 - a_n.

6.1 FBSM – First-BSM “safety-first” check

Logic: if any BSM is anomalous → classify sender as attacker.

function is_attacker = FBSM(a_vec)
    % a_vec is logical array of anomalous flags per BSM
    is_attacker = any(a_vec);
end


This yields high recall but more false positives (it is very sensitive). 

Physical Layer Plausibility Che…

6.2 MBSM – Majority-BSM check

Logic: if more than half of BSMs are anomalous → attacker.

function is_attacker = MBSM(a_vec)
    if isempty(a_vec)
        is_attacker = false;
        return;
    end
    frac_anom  = mean(a_vec);  % fraction of anomalous BSMs
    is_attacker = frac_anom > 0.5;
end


This gives very high precision but poor recall for attacks that misbehave only occasionally or late (e.g. Eventual Stop, random offsets). 

