A global threshold 
𝑇
T is set such that 99.7% of normal vehicles have final score above T. Any vehicle with final score < T is classified as attacker.

MATLAB implementation for one sequence:

function S = compute_WBSM_scores(a_vec, alpha)
    % a_vec: logical (1 = anomalous); we convert to BSM scores
    bsm_score = 1 - double(a_vec); % 1 normal, 0 anomalous
    N = numel(bsm_score);
    S = zeros(N,1);
    S_prev = 1; % start fully trusted
    for n = 1:N
        S_curr = (1-alpha)*S_prev + alpha*bsm_score(n);
        S(n) = S_curr;
        S_prev = S_curr;
    end
end


To determine classification, you need one final score per vehicle, per receiver. A simple choice is S_final = S(end) for that (receiver, sender) pair.

7. How to set α and the WBSM threshold

In the paper they sweep α ∈ {0.05, 0.10, …, 0.95} and choose the value that maximises the correct-classification rate (CCR).

7.1 Choosing the threshold T for WBSM

For a given α:

Run the WBSM score update on normal vehicles only.

For each (receiver, sender) pair that is truly normal (attack_type 0), compute its final score S_final.

Build the empirical distribution of S_final over normal vehicles.

Set threshold T as the 0.3rd percentile (lower 0.3%) – that gives a 99.7% one-sided confidence level (only 0.3% of normal vehicles fall below).

In MATLAB: T = quantile(S_final_normals, 0.003);

Then, when you evaluate on both attackers and normals, classify:

is_attacker_WBSM = (S_final < T);

7.2 Choosing α

For each α in the grid:

For each vehicle pair, compute S_final.

With threshold T (from normal vehicles only), label each vehicle as attacker/normal.

Compute CCR, precision, recall (see next section).

Pick α giving the highest CCR; record its precision and recall. This is what they report in Table 3 and Table 4.

Fig. 10 in the paper is simply precision–recall points as you vary α. 

Physical Layer Plausibility Che…

8. Computing metrics (CCR, precision, recall)

Definitions (vehicle-level): 

Physical Layer Plausibility Che…

True Positive (TP): attacker correctly detected as attacker.

False Positive (FP): normal vehicle incorrectly flagged as attacker.

False Negative (FN): attacker not detected.

True Negative (TN): normal correctly treated as normal.

Then:

CCR = (TP + TN) / (TP + TN + FP + FN)

Precision = TP / (TP + FP)

Recall = TP / (TP + FN)

In MATLAB:

function [CCR, prec, rec] = metrics(y_true, y_pred)
    % y_true: logical (1 attacker, 0 normal)
    % y_pred: logical (1 predicted attacker)
    TP = nnz( y_true &  y_pred);
    FP = nnz(~y_true &  y_pred);
    FN = nnz( y_true & ~y_pred);
    TN = nnz(~y_true & ~y_pred);
    
    CCR  = (TP + TN) / (TP + TN + FP + FN);
    prec = TP / max(1, (TP + FP));
    rec  = TP / max(1, (TP + FN));
end


To match per-attack tables (Table 3), you filter data for a given attack type and treat all vehicles with that attack type as attackers, the rest as normal. To get the overall table (Table 4), you merge all five attack types and evaluate once.

